---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Heading from "../../components/Heading.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import { HOWTO_GROUPS, REPO } from "../../consts";

// Intro and index for the how-to section. The guides themselves live in the
// `howto` content collection and render through HowToLayout; this page is
// hand-written because it is prose first and a list second.

const order = HOWTO_GROUPS.map((g) => g.name);
const all = (await getCollection("howto")).sort(
  (a, b) =>
    order.indexOf(a.data.group) - order.indexOf(b.data.group) ||
    a.data.order - b.data.order,
);
const groups = HOWTO_GROUPS.map((g) => ({
  ...g,
  items: all.filter((d) => d.data.group === g.name),
})).filter((g) => g.items.length);

// The task-oriented pages that live under /docs rather than here, because
// they are also the reference path into ABNF, actions and extension. Listed
// so this page is a complete index of the how-to quadrant, not a partial one.
const IN_DOCS = [
  {
    href: "/docs/abnf-grammars",
    title: "ABNF grammars",
    description: "Define a language fast using the RFC 5234 ABNF dialect.",
  },
  {
    href: "/docs/actions",
    title: "Attaching actions",
    description: "Turn a parse into a value — with builtins, named refs, or inline functions.",
  },
  {
    href: "/docs/extending",
    title: "Extending a grammar",
    description: "Add to a language that already parses, instead of forking it.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  wide
  title="How to"
  description="Task-oriented guides for the parsing problems that come up in practice — includes, expressions, options, line-oriented formats, custom tokens, errors, and debugging."
>
  {/* Indexed by Pagefind like the docs articles, so the section intro is
      findable from the search box; the card list is skipped below. */}
  <article class="howto-index" data-pagefind-body>
    <Heading level={1} id="how-to">How to</Heading>
    <p class="lede">
      Short guides for the problems that come up while writing a parser. Each
      one takes a single task, solves it against a running grammar, and says
      what it costs. They assume you have already parsed something — the
      <a href="/docs/quickstart">quickstart</a> is five minutes if you haven't.
    </p>

    <section class="intro">
      <Heading level={2} id="how-these-are-written">How these are written</Heading>
      <p>
        A guide here is task-shaped, not feature-shaped. The title is the thing
        you are trying to do; the page is how to do it and what it costs. Where
        a published package already solves the problem, the guide uses it and
        says so, rather than reimplementing it for the sake of an example —
        <a href="/docs/extending">extension is the point of the engine</a>, and
        it applies to the documentation too.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every code sample on these pages has been executed against the
        published packages, and the values in the <code>// =&gt;</code> comments
        are what the engine actually returned. Where a package behaves in a way
        that will surprise you, the guide says so instead of quietly avoiding
        it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The reference solutions are the real grammars. When a guide points at
        <a href="https://github.com/tabnas/csv" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@tabnas/csv</a>
        or
        <a href="https://github.com/tabnas/expr" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@tabnas/expr</a>,
        it is pointing at a grammar under a couple of thousand lines that you
        can read in one sitting — usually the fastest way to answer a question
        this section didn't anticipate.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="intro">
      <Heading level={2} id="before-you-write-a-grammar">Before you write a grammar</Heading>
      <p>
        The question to ask before any of this is <em>what already parses
        something close to my format?</em> A new grammar is the expensive
        answer, and usually the wrong one. The
        <a href="/docs/packages">packages page</a> lists what exists; the
        <a href="/comparisons">comparisons page</a> is honest about when
        another tool is the better answer entirely.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Heading level={2} id="guides">Guides</Heading>

    {groups.map((g) => (
      <section class="group" data-pagefind-ignore>
        <Heading level={3} id={g.name.toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, "-")}>{g.name}</Heading>
        <p class="group-blurb">{g.blurb}</p>
        <ul class="list">
          {g.items.map((d) => (
            <li>
              <a class="row" href={`/how-to/${d.id}`}>
                <span class="row-title">{d.data.title}</span>
                <span class="row-desc">{d.data.description}</span>
              </a>
              {d.data.packages.length > 0 && (
                <span class="pkgs">
                  {d.data.packages.map((p) => (
                    <a class="chip" href={REPO(p)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">@tabnas/{p}</a>
                  ))}
                </span>
              )}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    ))}

    <section class="group">
      <Heading level={3} id="also-in-the-docs">Also in the docs</Heading>
      <p class="group-blurb">
        Three task-oriented pages live under <a href="/docs">/docs</a> because
        they double as the way in to ABNF, actions and extension. They belong
        to this section as much as to that one.
      </p>
      <ul class="list">
        {IN_DOCS.map((d) => (
          <li>
            <a class="row" href={d.href}>
              <span class="row-title">{d.title}</span>
              <span class="row-desc">{d.description}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="intro">
      <Heading level={2} id="if-none-of-these-fit">If none of these fit</Heading>
      <p>
        The <a href="/docs/rule-table">rule table reference</a> is the whole
        format in one page, and is short enough to read end to end. The
        <a href="/playground">playground</a> runs a grammar in the browser, so
        a guess can be checked in a few seconds. Failing both,
        <a href="/community">open an issue</a> — a question this section should
        have answered is a bug in this section.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .howto-index { max-width: 60rem; }
  .lede { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 1.1rem; max-width: 46rem; }
  .intro p { max-width: 46rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .intro :global(h2) { margin-top: 2.5rem; }

  .group { margin-top: 2.25rem; }
  .group :global(h3) { margin-bottom: 0.35rem; }
  .group-blurb { color: var(--ink-mute); max-width: 46rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; font-size: 0.95rem; }

  .list { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 0.6rem; }
  .row {
    display: block;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .row:hover { border-color: var(--border-strong); }
  .row-title { display: block; color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600; }
  .row-desc { display: block; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.92rem; margin-top: 0.15rem; }

  .pkgs { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.35rem; margin: 0.4rem 0 0 0.15rem; }
  .chip {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--ink-mute); background: var(--surface-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 999px; padding: 0.1rem 0.55rem;
  }
  .chip:hover { color: var(--primary); border-color: var(--border-strong); }
</style>
